# factory/config.env — tunables for the 24/7 bounded-autonomy harness.
#
# Sourced by every factory component. Keep this file free of secrets:
# the API key lives in ~/.factory/env (mode 600), never in the repo.
#
# Every value here is a SAFETY BOUND, not a performance knob. Raising a cap
# raises the amount of unattended spend and unattended change. Treat edits to
# this file the way you would treat edits to a production rollout config.

# Every setting below uses the ${VAR:-default} form so a single run can be
# overridden from the environment without editing this file:
#   MAX_PARALLEL=1 ./factory/dispatch.sh
# The file is still the source of truth; the environment is the override.
#
# ---- Parallelism -----------------------------------------------------------
# Max concurrent dispatcher workers. Each worker = 1 worktree + 1 claude loop.
MAX_PARALLEL="${MAX_PARALLEL:-3}"

# Heavy parallelism is confined to this UTC window so the machine stays
# responsive for interactive work during the day. Loops themselves are allowed
# 24/7; outside the window the dispatcher clamps to DAY_MAX_PARALLEL.
NIGHT_START="${NIGHT_START:-22}"
NIGHT_END="${NIGHT_END:-7}"
DAY_MAX_PARALLEL="${DAY_MAX_PARALLEL:-1}"

# ---- Loop bounds -----------------------------------------------------------
# A loop that has not passed its gate within these bounds is BLOCKED, full stop.
# More attempts on a stuck agent is pure burn, so these are hard ceilings.
DEFAULT_MAX_ATTEMPTS="${DEFAULT_MAX_ATTEMPTS:-10}"
DEFAULT_MAX_MINUTES="${DEFAULT_MAX_MINUTES:-240}"

# Stop early if the gate emits a byte-identical failure this many times in a
# row: identical output means the agent is not learning from the gate.
SAME_ERROR_LIMIT="${SAME_ERROR_LIMIT:-3}"

# ---- Dispatch tier allowlist -----------------------------------------------
# Which tier labels the dispatcher is permitted to pick up, space-separated.
# This is a SECOND gate on top of the hard tier rules in dispatch.sh (which can
# NEVER dispatch tier:T0 or tier:T1 no matter what appears here) — it exists so
# a stage can be narrower than the code's ceiling without editing code.
#
# STAGE 1 POLICY: tier:T3 ONLY. T2 is deliberately left out: T3 is the tier
# whose gate is a self-contained subproject build/test, so a bad T3 change
# cannot reach consensus code. T2 stays parked until T3 has produced a track
# record of merged PRs.
DISPATCH_TIERS="${DISPATCH_TIERS:-tier:T3}"

# ---- Merge gating ----------------------------------------------------------
# T2 auto-merge stays false by policy. See merge.sh: it refuses to merge
# ANYTHING while branch protection with required status checks is absent,
# regardless of these flags.
#
# 2026-08-03: branch protection VERIFIED PRESENT on master — required checks
# "gate" and "full", enforce_admins on, force-pushes disabled. That was the sole
# precondition on T3 auto-merge, so MERGE_T3 moves from the placeholder string
# to a plain true. Merging still additionally requires ENABLE_MERGE=true, the
# agent-reviewed label (>=2/3 review lenses PASS), no needs-human label, and
# green CI.
MERGE_T2="${MERGE_T2:-false}"
MERGE_T3="${MERGE_T3:-true}"

# ---- Stage switches (both default OFF) -------------------------------------
# Stage 1 turns the dispatcher on; Stage 2 turns merging on. Each component also
# refuses to act unless its own flag is true, so a timer alone cannot start
# autonomous work.
ENABLE_DISPATCH="${ENABLE_DISPATCH:-false}"
ENABLE_MERGE="${ENABLE_MERGE:-false}"

# ---- Daily spawn budget ----------------------------------------------------
# Per-token cost is not observable from a headless `claude -p` call, so the
# factory bounds what it CAN count: agent spawns per UTC day. Every spawn
# appends one line to ~/.factory/spend-YYYYMMDD; dispatch.sh, loop.sh, and
# review.sh all reserve against it and REFUSE to start once the cap is hit.
# The date is in the filename, so the budget resets at 00:00 UTC by itself.
#
# WHAT COUNTS AS ONE SPAWN: one dispatched worker loop, one gate-fixing attempt
# inside that loop, or one review lens. All three are a `claude -p` process.
#
# MAPPING THE CAP TO DOLLARS (estimate, not a measurement — treat as an order of
# magnitude and re-derive from the Console once real runs exist):
#   worker attempt  ~ $0.50 - $2.00   (long context, many tool calls, up to 240m)
#   review lens     ~ $0.10 - $0.40   (one diff + one issue body, no tools)
# Assume a blended ~$0.75/spawn: DAILY_SPAWN_CAP=40 is roughly $30/day, ~$900
# if a runaway ran unnoticed for a month.
#
# A worked example of where 40 goes: 6 T3 issues x 1 dispatch = 6, plus up to
# 10 attempts each = 60 — so the cap bites partway through the first full pass.
# That is intentional for the first stage: the first thing you want to learn is
# whether the loops work at all, not how much they can spend unattended.
#
# THIS IS NOT A BILLING CONTROL. The Anthropic Console monthly cap remains the
# hard backstop; this exists so a runaway is stopped in minutes by the machine
# that started it rather than in days by a billing alert.
DAILY_SPAWN_CAP="${DAILY_SPAWN_CAP:-40}"

# Where the dated ledgers and their lock live.
SPEND_DIR="${SPEND_DIR:-$HOME/.factory}"

# ---- Review scope ----------------------------------------------------------
# A reviewer can only judge what it is shown, so these knobs decide COVERAGE.
# Widening them costs tokens; narrowing them costs review quality. Neither
# direction is free, and neither may ever turn into "the reviewer passed it
# because it did not look": review.sh reports INCONCLUSIVE, never PASS, when it
# cannot review.
#
# Files whose diff BODY is withheld from the review prompt because they are
# generated or vendored: a 4662-line package-lock.json is not reviewable by a
# human or a model, and it crowds out the code that is. These are git pathspec
# globs, applied with :(exclude,glob).
#
# The names of excluded files are STILL shown to every lens, and every
# package.json / Cargo.toml diff is reproduced in full in its own section, so a
# dependency change stays reviewable even though the lockfile body is dropped.
REVIEW_EXCLUDE_GLOBS="${REVIEW_EXCLUDE_GLOBS:-**/package-lock.json **/pnpm-lock.yaml **/yarn.lock **/*.min.js **/*.min.css **/dist/** **/build/** **/node_modules/** **/*.map **/*snapshot.json}"

# Manifests whose diff is always passed in full, never excluded, never
# truncated. This is what keeps requirement "no silent coverage loss" true for
# the one class of change a lockfile exclusion would otherwise hide.
REVIEW_MANIFEST_GLOBS="${REVIEW_MANIFEST_GLOBS:-**/package.json **/Cargo.toml}"

# Hard ceilings on review-diff size AFTER exclusion, whichever binds first.
# Truncation happens at file boundaries and is DECLARED to the lens ("diff
# truncated at N lines; omitted files: …") and logged, because silent truncation
# reads as full coverage when it is not.
#
# NEITHER CAP IS AN execve() DEFENCE. The prompt is delivered to `claude -p` on
# stdin as a file redirect, so MAX_ARG_STRLEN cannot be reached at any diff size
# (that was the #86 E2BIG bug, and the redirect is what actually fixed it).
# These caps exist purely so the reviewer gets material it can actually hold.
#
# 2026-08-05: raised 1500 -> 2500. At 1500 the docs PR #85 (2256 post-exclusion
# lines) had `token-model.md` truncated out of the review, and the reviewer
# consequently produced TWO FALSE FINDINGS from the missing file — a √stake vs
# stake "discrepancy" that the truncated page actually resolves, and a
# "only one chain-check directive exists" claim when there are ten. Partial
# coverage does not just lose findings, it manufactures them. 2500 gives that
# class of PR full coverage.
MAX_REVIEW_DIFF_LINES="${MAX_REVIEW_DIFF_LINES:-2500}"

# Byte ceiling, because lines are a bad proxy for size. A diff of
# generated-but-not-globbed content (one-line JSON blobs, embedded base64, a
# minified file that dodged REVIEW_EXCLUDE_GLOBS) can be a handful of lines and
# still megabytes. Without this, such a diff would pass the line cap and produce
# an unreviewably huge prompt. 200000 bytes is ~50k tokens of diff.
MAX_REVIEW_DIFF_BYTES="${MAX_REVIEW_DIFF_BYTES:-200000}"

# Wall-clock ceiling per review lens (seconds). A lens killed by this timeout
# exits 124 and is recorded as ERROR — never as FAIL.
REVIEW_LENS_TIMEOUT="${REVIEW_LENS_TIMEOUT:-900}"

# ---- Guards ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Below this much free disk (GB), stop dispatching new work.
MIN_FREE_DISK_GB="${MIN_FREE_DISK_GB:-30}"

# How long all loops back off after a rate-limit / overloaded response (min).
BACKOFF_MINUTES="${BACKOFF_MINUTES:-60}"

# ---- Paths -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared cargo cache: worktrees reuse one target dir to avoid re-building the
# whole SDK per task. Concurrent cargo invocations against one target dir race,
# so all cargo runs are serialized behind a flock (see worktree.sh).
SHARED_CARGO_TARGET="${SHARED_CARGO_TARGET:-$HOME/shared-target}"
CARGO_LOCKFILE="${CARGO_LOCKFILE:-$HOME/.factory/cargo.lock}"

# Kill switch: if this file exists, every component exits before doing work.
STOP_FILE="${STOP_FILE:-$HOME/STOP_FACTORY}"

# Back-off sentinel, written by watchdog.sh and honoured by loop.sh.
BACKOFF_FILE="${BACKOFF_FILE:-$HOME/.factory/backoff}"

# Base branch new worktrees are cut from. Move to master once master is green.
BASE_BRANCH="${BASE_BRANCH:-master}"

# Upstream repo (used for gh calls; empty = infer from the checkout).
# shellcheck disable=SC2034  # read by gh_repo_args() in lib/common.sh; shellcheck
# cannot see that use because common.sh sources this file via a variable path.
FACTORY_REPO=""
